rt-dot sig\" cx=\"373.82540540540543\" cy=\"111.45945945945948\" r=\"6.5\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></g><g class=\"pt\" data-tip=\"Forecast said 99%&amp;#10;It rained 87% of the time&amp;#10;Based on 147 days&amp;#10;outside the perfect-forecast range - real miscalibration\"><circle class=\"chart-dot sig\" cx=\"409.13428571428574\" cy=\"65.04761904761904\" r=\"6.5\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></g><text class=\"chart-label\" x=\"235.0\" y=\"422\" font-size=\"12.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">What the forecast promised</text><text class=\"chart-label\" x=\"15\" y=\"200.0\" font-size=\"12.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"rotate(-90 15 200.0)\">How often it actually rained</text></svg>\n<figcaption>Green band = where a <b>flawless</b> forecast would still land, given\nhow few days we have. Red dots fall outside it, so those are real errors rather\nthan noise. The faint horizontal line is climatology\n(50%).</figcaption></figure>\n","season":"\n<h2 id=\"season\">Does the season matter?</h2>\n<p>In Nuku&#x27;alofa the forecast is most skilful in\n<b>winter</b> (0.32) and least in\n<b>autumn</b> (0.21).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\">\n<table><thead><tr><th>Season</th><th class=\"num\">Days</th>\n<th class=\"num\">Rain days</th><th class=\"num\">Skill score</th>\n<th class=\"num\">Avg error</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>autumn</td><td class='num'>173</td><td class='num'>43%</td><td class='num'>0.208</td><td class='num'>12.1%</td></tr><tr><td>spring</td><td class='num'>205</td><td class='num'>55%</td><td class='num'>0.229</td><td class='num'>9.1%</td></tr><tr><td>summer</td><td class='num'>174</td><td class='num'>40%</td><td class='num'>0.291</td><td class='num'>8.8%</td></tr><tr><td>winter</td><td class='num'>167</td><td class='num'>59%</td><td class='num'>0.320</td><td class='num'>6.8%</td></tr></tbody></table>\n</div>\n<p class=\"muted\">Four-way splits of a two-year record are thin, so read the\nordering as indicative. Coarser binning is used here than for the annual curve\nfor 